# NOBORU GAMIFICATION RULE

Version: 1.0

Priority: HIGH

Status: AUTHORITATIVE

This rule governs achievements, leagues, games, shop systems, and all motivational mechanics within Noboru.

Every badge, rank, reward, challenge, event, and cosmetic economy interaction must follow this document, docs/game-design.md, and docs/gamification.md.

architecture.mdc defines the prime rule: gamification reads educational progress; it never owns it.

⸻

PURPOSE

The purpose of this rule is to ensure:

* Gamification strengthens learning instead of replacing it
* Rewards remain fair, transparent, and non-manipulative
* Educational progress stays authoritative
* Game systems remain isolated behind their feature modules
* Premium and cosmetic systems never create educational advantage
* Long-term engagement without dark patterns

Motivation must support mastery.

Never undermine it.

⸻

REQUIREMENTS

Authority Model

* Educational progress is authoritative
* Gamification systems read progress from learning domains
* Forbidden: gamification tables becoming the source of truth for mastery, SRS, lesson completion, or JLPT advancement
* Forbidden: educational services depending on league rank, shop inventory, or achievement state

References

* docs/game-design.md defines game objectives, forbidden design, and activity structure
* docs/gamification.md defines reward systems, leagues, achievements, and economy rules
* When documents conflict with architecture.mdc on ownership, architecture.mdc wins

Feature Ownership

* achievements owns achievement definitions, unlock logic presentation, and achievement UI
* leagues owns ranking presentation and seasonal league workflows
* games owns educational mini-games that teach
* shop owns cosmetics, convenience items, and non-educational purchases

Educational Games

* Every game must teach
* Fun without learning is a failure
* Learning without reasonable enjoyment is a failure
* Games consume content from educational domains; they do not own vocabulary, kanji, or grammar data

Rewards

* Rewards must map to real learning behavior
* Never grant educational advantage through payment or randomness
* Premium may offer convenience and customization only

Engagement Ethics

* No guilt messaging
* No fear-of-loss streak manipulation as primary retention
* No fake urgency or artificial scarcity for learning outcomes
* Notifications must be respectful and actionable

Layering

* Gamification services orchestrate reads and writes within their domain
* Progress reads go through educational services or repositories as defined by architecture
* UI in gamification features must not query educational tables directly

Offline

* Non-critical gamification may degrade gracefully offline
* Core learning must remain functional without gamification sync

⸻

CODING STANDARDS

Services

* achievements.service.ts evaluates unlock conditions from read-only educational inputs
* leagues.service.ts computes rank from approved metrics, not hidden heuristics in UI
* games.service.ts assembles sessions from educational content services
* shop.service.ts validates purchases, ownership, and entitlements

Data Flow

Educational Domain

↓

Read model or service adapter

↓

Gamification service

↓

Gamification repository

↓

Gamification tables

Types

* Separate gamification types from educational domain types
* Use explicit mapping layers when combining data for UI

UI

* Present progress and rewards clearly
* Never obscure learning state behind game metaphors alone
* One primary action per screen, per uiux.mdc

Testing

* Test unlock logic against representative educational inputs
* Test that educational services do not import gamification services

⸻

SUCCESS CRITERIA

Gamification work is successful when:

* Learning remains the primary user feeling
* Educational progress remains authoritative
* Rewards are understandable and tied to real improvement
* Games measurably reinforce recall, recognition, or production
* Shop and premium systems do not alter learning quality
* Feature modules remain isolated and testable
* docs/game-design.md and docs/gamification.md remain accurate

⸻

FAILURE CRITERIA

Gamification quality is degraded when any of the following occur:

* Mastery or review state stored only in gamification tables
* Educational services importing shop or league modules
* Games that entertain without teaching
* Pay-to-learn advantages
* Streak systems that punish learning gaps coercively
* Reward spam or infinite popup loops
* Hidden randomness affecting educational outcomes
* Cross-feature UI coupling between unrelated game systems

When failure criteria appear, remove gamification authority over learning before adding new rewards.

⸻

EXAMPLES

Correct unlock flow

achievementsService.evaluate(userId) reads completed lesson count from learningService.getSummary(userId) and writes achievement unlock rows only.

Correct game session

gamesService.startVocabularyDrill(userId, level) requests items from vocabularyService.getSessionItems().

Correct shop item

Cosmetic trail effect with no impact on lesson access or review scheduling.

Incorrect authority

Updating user kanji mastery only inside leagues progression tables.

Incorrect dependency

learningService imports shopService to gate lesson access.

Incorrect game

Timed slot machine with Japanese icons but no targeted learning objective.

⸻

ANTI-PATTERNS

Gamification owning educational progress

Educational domains reading league or shop state to unlock content

Grinding loops with no learning benefit

Manipulative streak loss messaging

Paid boosts to SRS outcomes or JLPT placement

Hardcoded achievement thresholds scattered in UI

Direct educational database queries inside games components

Reward popups that interrupt active lessons

Cross-feature reward logic duplicated in multiple services

Using engagement metrics as a substitute for mastery metrics

END OF gamification.mdc
